WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.109
Zero Trust responds to changes in how organizations use technology.

00:00:04.109 --> 00:00:08.118
Employees work from offices, homes, customer sites, and mobile locations.

00:00:08.118 --> 00:00:12.527
Applications may run in data centers, public cloud platforms, software services,

00:00:12.527 --> 00:00:13.730
and partner environments.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.135
Data also moves across organizational boundaries.

00:00:16.135 --> 00:00:19.743
Teams collaborate with suppliers, contractors, customers, and service providers.

00:00:19.743 --> 00:00:24.554
A simple distinction between inside and outside no longer describes where important

00:00:24.554 --> 00:00:26.959
work occurs or where risk exists.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.964
Traditional perimeter controls remain valuable,

00:00:28.964 --> 00:00:34.977
but network location alone provides limited evidence about whether an access request should be allowed.

00:00:34.977 --> 00:00:40.589
A request from an internal address can still involve an unmanaged device, excessive privilege,

00:00:40.589 --> 00:00:42.995
or an identity showing unexpected behavior.

00:00:42.995 --> 00:00:46.202
Cloud and software-as-a-service adoption also change enforcement points.

00:00:46.202 --> 00:00:51.413
Users may connect directly to applications without passing through a traditional enterprise network,

00:00:51.413 --> 00:00:55.422
so identity, application policy, and service telemetry become increasingly important.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:59.832
Remote work and mobile devices require security decisions across changing networks.

00:00:59.832 --> 00:01:06.246
Organizations need a consistent way to evaluate access whether the request originates in a headquarters office,

00:01:06.246 --> 00:01:09.453
a home network, or a managed mobile connection.

00:01:09.453 --> 00:01:14.264
Zero Trust supports risk management by reducing reliance on broad, persistent access.

00:01:14.264 --> 00:01:19.074
It encourages organizations to grant access to specific resources based on business

00:01:19.074 --> 00:01:23.083
need and to reassess that access as relevant conditions change.

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:27.493
The strategy can also limit the effect of a security failure.

00:01:27.493 --> 00:01:30.700
If one identity, device, or segment is affected,

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:36.313
narrowly scoped permissions and stronger policy boundaries can reduce unnecessary access to unrelated resources.

00:01:36.313 --> 00:01:39.921
Organizations adopt Zero Trust gradually to improve verification, visibility,

00:01:39.921 --> 00:01:42.326
and control across this distributed environment.

00:01:42.326 --> 00:01:46.736
It complements other security practices rather than replacing identity management, monitoring,

00:01:46.736 --> 00:01:48.740
network security, or incident response.
